 The plan is deterministic per learner per day (seeded by user id and date). Due questions drop out of the
 plan as they are answered. Learner ids always come from the verified JWT.
 
+**Timed daily mock (exam-service)**
+
+The mock reuses the exam engine instead of adding a second timer. `POST /api/attempts/daily?category=` builds a
+private `DAILY` exam for the learner for today and starts it through the normal `AttemptService.start()`, so the
+server-owned deadline, auto-submit, palette, scoring and result page all apply.
+
+```
+ POST /api/attempts/daily?category=JAVA_INTERVIEW
+   today's mock already exists?  yes -> resume it, or report it completed (one mock per learner, category and day)
+   no -> progress-service GET /internal/daily-plan  (due revisions, weak topics, new topics)
+         if progress-service is down -> seeded-random mix of the category's topics instead
+      -> create exam kind=DAILY, owner=learner, date=today: 10 questions, 1.5 min each (15 min), no negative marking
+      -> AttemptService.start()  -> /attempts/:id  (existing exam screen)
+ finished or timed out -> exam-service POST /internal/results to progress-service (idempotent on attemptId)
+```
+
+| Endpoint | Purpose |
+|---|---|
+| `POST /api/attempts/daily?category=` | start today's mock, or resume / return its status |
+| `GET /api/attempts/daily/today?category=` | `NOT_STARTED`, `IN_PROGRESS` (with `expiresAt`) or `COMPLETED` (with score) |
+| `GET /internal/daily-plan?userId=&category=` (progress-service) | the plan the mock is built from |
+| `POST /internal/results` (progress-service) | answered questions of a finished attempt; feeds revision boxes, topic accuracy, streak |
+
+Daily exams are hidden from `/api/exams`, the public stats and the admin exam list. Concurrent "start" clicks
+cannot create two attempts (unique index plus a row lock). Results delivery is eventually consistent: a failed
+call leaves the attempt `PENDING` and a scheduler retries it every minute for 24 hours. Only answered,
+auto-gradable questions are reported, so a timed-out mock does not drag accuracy down. Questions due for
+revision are drawn from their topic (not the exact question) for now.
+
